def polygon_edge(point, next_point, final_point):
    head = (next_point[0] - point[0], next_point[1] - point[1])
    tail = (final_point[0] - point[0], final_point[1] - point[1])
    return cross(head, tail)

def is_convex_polygon(points):
    n = len(points)
    if n < 3:
        return False

    # Close the polygon by repeating the first point at the end
    for i in range(n):
        p = points[i]
        q = points[(i + 1) % n]
        r = points[(i + 2) % n]
        c = polygon_edge(p, q, r)
        if i == 0:
            sign = 1 if c > 0 else -1
        elif sign * c < 0:
            return False
    return True
```

The polygon is convex if and only all consecutive triples of vertices point in the same rough (顺时针 or 逆时针) direction, with no three lying on a line. To check this, the code computes a 2D cross product between the edge being stepped and the vector to the next vertex. A change of sign in this product means the interior of the polygon folds back on itself, while a zero means those three points are collinear. The last two characters matter most: following two edges of the type described in the example, (0, 0) to (2, 0) and to (1, 1), gives a cross product of -2, and following the next edge (to (2, 2)) gives a cross product of 2, so a sign change tells the function the polygon folds and the answer is False.

