The function compares points by squared Euclidean distance to avoid a square root that would not affect the ordering.

It starts with the first point in the list, and then loops through the rest: whenever a point has a smaller squared distance than the closest point found so far, it becomes the new closest. Ties are resolved by returning whichever of the two tieing points was encountered first, because a later point would only replace the stored one when its distance is strictly smaller and never when it is equal. That is why the first point in the list is never overwritten, and that is why a point with the same minimal distance that comes later in the list is never the answer. So the requirement to return the first point that achieves the smallest distance comes out for free.

