def equal_under_rotation(grid1, grid2):
        if len(grid1) == 0:
            return True
        h = len(grid1)
        w = len(grid1[0])
        if h != len(grid2) or w != len(grid2[0]):
            return False
        for _ in range(4):
            if all(grid1[i][j] == grid2[i][j] for i in range(h) for j in range(w)):
                return True
            grid1 = [[grid1[w - j - 1][i] for j in range(w)] for i in range(h)]
        return False
```

The code checks whether the two grids agree after 0, 90, 180, or 270-degree rotations, returning True if any rotation matches exactly. The 90-degree rotation about the top-left corner is implemented by taking grid1, rotating it one step, then repeating that three more times inside the loop.

A perfect match on the first pass of the loop signals success, because even 0 degrees counts. Rotation is accomplished by swapping the row and column indices and reversing order in one direction. With board of size $ h \times w $, the loop rotates four times and makes the first check before the rotation is complete, so the loop's heading implements the four rotations in one block. The dimensions must agree before the body begins. Empty boards are handled by the first branch, in which a rotation test makes no sense and equality holds by fiat.

