The function is called at `(0, 0)` with a memo: `@lru_cache(maxsize=None)`. It returns 1 when reaching the cell `(m - 1, n - 1)`. From each cell it otherwise explores the cell below, adding whatever paths come from it, and the cell to the right, and returns the total. A blocked cell anywhere on a path blocks that path, so the two required checks at the top, whether the start or the finish is blocked, are the only parts of the input that need explicit access; the rest of the process happens in the recursive backtracking.

It is clearly a count of paths rather than a path-finding algorithm, and although it traverses vastly more cells than needed it does no unnecessary work because the only paths it walks are those really present, and each is counted exactly once. There is also no flood-fill step, and it needs no table of counts, only the one recursive call and its memorisation. Paths can blossom except when a single obstacle runs from the start to the finish, which is exactly the condition of the empty grid where both asserts of 0 are needed.

