def parse_env_line(line):
    equals = line.find('=')
    if equals == -1:
        raise ValueError('no = in line')
    key = line[:equals]
    value_part = line[equals + 1:]
    if len(value_part) < 1 or (value_part[0] != value_part[-1]):
        return (key, value_part)
    start_quote = value_part[0]
    value_length = len(value_part)
    if value_length < 2 or value_part[-1] != start_quote:
        return (key, value_part)
    return (key, value_part[1:-1])
```

The line is first split between the key and value at the first equals sign, since that is where an env line is guaranteed to split. The value portion is then examined to see whether it is enclosed in quotes. The value is quoted when it has at least two characters and its first and last characters are the same quote: double or single. In that case, the two enclosing characters are stripped. If the value is not quoted, or is quoted but only one character long (which can happen with an empty quoted value such as 'KEY=""'), the value is returned as it is. The characters of the key itself are never quoted, never include whitespace and never include an equals sign, so they are safe to keep verbatim.

