```python
def elevator_sweep_stops(start_floor, direction, requests, top_floor):
    stops = set(requests)
    if start_floor in stops:
        stops.remove(start_floor)
        stops.add(start_floor)

    floor = start_floor
    result = []
    while True:
        if floor in stops:
            result.append(floor)
            stops.remove(floor)
        if direction == 1:
            if floor >= top_floor:
                break
            floor += 1
        else:
            if floor <= 1:
                break
            floor -= 1
    return result
```

The elevator starts at start_floor and moves one floor at a time in the direction given. When it reaches a floor that is a requested one, it stops; the stop is added to the answer and the floor is removed from the set so that it is not visited again. On the first sweep through the list, start_floor is checked first, by adding and then removing it, so that it gets scanned again if it was already in requests.

The loop continues until the top floor or floor 1 is reached and then one final step is taken beyond that to make the check at floor 1 or top_floor happen and to ensure the sweep really stops there.

A set is used for the remaining requests so that a lookup can know whether to stop at a particular floor in constant time. Any stopping floor is placed on the answer list just before it is removed from the set.

